  "slug": "blastomere-central-spindle-6ae1",
  "summary": "Three preprints and one published study refine how developing cells restrict fate and cell-cycle programs.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is how developing cells restrict fate and cell-cycle programs as competence narrows. In C. elegans, blastomeres remain competent for muscle fate until late 16E, with LIN-54/DREAM and MET-2 restricting this plasticity. A separate C. elegans study shows DREAM-mediated repression of zen-4, cyk-4 and spd-1 marks the endomitotic transition in the intestine and blocks cytokinesis. In Capitella teleta, Wnt signaling and TALE/FOX transcript asymmetry are linked to blastomere identity and neural fate during spiral cleavage. In planarians, canonical Wnt signaling controls early proliferation and differentiation of neural, muscle and parenchymal tissues. The new element is DREAM acting on both commitment and cytokinetic gene programs, while the Wnt studies extend control to proliferation and tissue differentiation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
